# name  rec  ticks  slot  mods  survives  equipped  carried  equipset  requires
#
# name      identifies the ROW, and names the golden file. Unique.
# rec       which input recording to replay, without the .rec suffix. NOT the same thing as the
#           name: a recording is just a per-tick input stream, and Replay validates only the
#           format, the engine version and the mod list -- never the save it is replayed against.
#           So one recording can drive several fixtures, and 'death' below is 'patrol' again on a
#           weaker character. Reusing the file beats copying it; a copy drifts in silence.
# ticks     how long to run.
# slot      which save game tests/make-fixture.sh writes that the run boots from.
#           1 = movement, 2 = combat, 3 = beatdown, 4 = death, 5 = death with every penalty
#           on, 6 = naked, on a bare test map that hands it something to wear, 7 = slot 2's
#           character in an empty room, so a set swap is the only thing that can happen,
#           8 = naked, carrying one shirt, in that same room, to drag it onto the chest slot.
# mods      EXTRA mods appended to the default list, or '-' for none. Mods in tests/mods/ are
#           linked into the data path by tests/run-replays.sh. Order matters -- later mods
#           override earlier ones, so an override has to land last, which appending guarantees.
#           NOTE a recording's header records the mod list it was made under and Replay REFUSES a
#           mismatch, so changing this column means the row needs a recording whose header agrees.
# survives  'yes' if the player must be alive at the end, 'no' if the player must die.
#           Both directions are enforced. P0.5e added this check in one direction only, because
#           two fixtures had rotted into corpses partway through -- patrol died at tick 1101 of
#           2000 and beatdown at 1186 of 2956, and both were green, because 'requires' only asks
#           whether an event EVER fired and every one of them had fired before the death.
#           The other direction matters too: the death penalty runs only when the player dies, so
#           a 'no' row is the only way that code is reachable under test at all. (It lived in
#           MenuInventory::logic() when this column was written; P1.3b moved it to
#           applyDeathPenalty(), driven from GameStatePlay's tick. The reason the column exists is
#           unchanged -- the penalty is still only reachable through a death.) A 'no' row that
#           stops dying is a silently disarmed test, exactly like a 'yes' row that starts.
# equipped  how many equipment slots must still hold something when the run ends.
#           This is the column that speaks to P1.3's own stated failure mode -- "a subtle error
#           means players lose gear" -- in a number a reviewer can read, rather than in a hex
#           digest that can only say "different".
#           Pinned on EVERY row, including the naked ones, because 0 is a claim too: 'autoequip'
#           below is only a test at all because its fixture starts at 0 and must end at 1.
#           melee and beatdown are 6 at both ends, which is the assertion that 2956 ticks of
#           combat do not quietly cost the player a slot.
# carried   how many carried slots must still hold something when the run ends.
#           Read this WITH 'equipped'; the pair is the check and neither number is worth much
#           alone. P1.3d-4 moves the item storage out of the menus and the way that goes wrong is
#           one copy of the data quietly becoming two. Measured on 'invdrag' by sabotaging
#           MenuInventory::activate() in each direction:
#               the item moves      equipped 0->1  carried 1->0   the only combination that passes
#               the item is COPIED  equipped 0->1  carried stays 1
#               the item is LOST    equipped stays 0  carried 1->0
#           A digest moves in all three and cannot tell them apart.
#           The other rows are pinned too, and several say something specific: 'melee' ends on 1
#           because it looted a stack, 'death' on 1 because the currency stack survives the
#           penalty, and 'deathfull' on 2 because the test mod's random-item branch destroyed one
#           of the three stacks it started with.
#           KNOWN PLATFORM SPLIT on 'deathfull': x86-64 Linux reports 3, not 2. The penalty removes
#           ONE UNIT of a randomly drawn stack, and that fixture carries [shirt, pants, 750
#           currency]. macOS and arm64 Linux draw index 0 and the shirt's slot empties; x86-64 can
#           only still hold 3 stacks if it drew the currency, since everything else has quantity 1.
#           Same family as the 'die' entry above -- the number here is right and the platforms
#           genuinely differ. See .github/workflows/main.yml. Do NOT change the 2.
#           'beatdown' splits the same way and was INVISIBLE until run-replays.sh stopped
#           short-circuiting: it fails its coverage requirement on Linux, so its gear numbers were
#           never reached. macOS loots nothing there and ends on 0; x86-64 Linux loots twice and
#           ends on 1. One divergence was masking another. Do NOT change the 0 either.
# equipset  which equipment set must be active when the run ends.
#           A different question from 'equipped': that one is how much gear EXISTS, this one is
#           how much of it the character is WEARING. MenuInventory::isEquipSlotActive() returns
#           false for every slot whose set is not the active one, so the two can disagree
#           completely -- 'equipswap' below ends on 6 and 2, six items held and none of them worn.
#           Every fixture loads on set 1; only that row changes it.
# requires  simevents counters that must be NON-ZERO for this row, or '-' for none.
#           This column exists because P0.5b shipped a recording called 'attack' that never
#           attacked, and three matching digests could not tell anyone. A digest proves the world
#           changed; only these say WHICH code ran.
#
#           'map_event' is on every row as a canary for a map having loaded, and it works, but
#           NOT for the reason this comment used to give. It said the event is emitted during map
#           load. It is not: EventManager.cpp:1115 emits SFX_MAP_EVENT only for a map event that
#           carries a soundfx component, and it happens that every campaign map has one on load.
#           A map with no sound-carrying event reports map_event=0 while running perfectly well,
#           which is what tests/mods/test_autoequip hit; that map now carries a soundfx so the
#           canary means the same thing on every row.
#           The canary earns its place either way: a zero is what every Linux CI run reported
#           until P0.5c, hashing an empty world and passing.
#
#           'die' on the melee row is worth a word, because it does not detect what its name says.
#           On x86-64 Linux that row reports die=0 AND critdie=2: the same two enemies die, but a
#           critical killing blow emits SFX_CRITDIE instead. So this entry is really asserting
#           that the killing blow is not critical -- which is an accident, and is also currently
#           the only thing in the suite that notices macOS and Linux simulate differently.
#           Leave it. Relaxing it to 'die or critdie' would make the label honest by deleting the
#           detector.

smoke     smoke     600   1  -  yes  0  0  1  map_event,step
patrol    patrol    2000  1  -  yes  0  0  1  map_event,step
melee     melee     2956  2  -  yes  6  1  1  map_event,attack,hit,die,step,levelup,loot,power
beatdown  beatdown  2956  3  -  yes  6  0  1  map_event,attack,hit,step,power,hazard_hit,lowhp_start,lowhp_stop
death     patrol    1400  4  -  no   0  1  1  map_event,step,hit,lowhp_start
deathfull deathfull 1400  5  test_deathpenalty  no   0  2  1  map_event,step,hit,lowhp_start
autoequip autoequip 900   6  test_autoequip     yes  1  0  1  map_event,step
equipswap equipswap 900   7  test_equipswap     yes  6  0  2  map_event,step

# invdrag WAS a row here (900 ticks, slot 8, mods test_equipswap, survives yes, requires
# map_event,step, expected equipped=1 carried=0) -- dropped by P1.4c, not weakened. It replays a
# mouse drag from a carried slot onto the chest equipment slot, which only ever worked because
# every server before P1.4c embedded the FULL client stack minus rendering: MenuInventory's own
# widget hit-testing (slot rectangles from menus/inventory.txt) and its drag-state machine
# (MenuInventory::activate(), drag_prev_slot/drag_prev_src) ran the mouse coordinates into a real
# equip exactly as the client would, with nothing ever drawn. P1.4c's whole point was to stop
# being "client minus rendering" -- MenuManager and MenuInventory are gone from flare-server, on
# purpose, and with them the only code that could turn this recording's mouse input into an equip.
#
# MEASURED, not assumed: replaying invdrag.rec against the pre-P1.4c server (commit 6a531b36,
# still carrying MenuManager/MenuInventory) gives equipped=1 carried=0 -- passes. The same
# recording against P1.4c's server gives equipped=0 carried=1 -- the item never leaves CARRIED,
# because nothing server-side ever reads a mouse-drag as an equip action. This is not a bug in
# the P1.4c rewrite to chase further; it is the rewrite doing exactly what it says on the tin.
#
# There is no sim-side substitute to port this to, the same way checkHotspots() (point-and-click
# map interaction) and RESPEC's stat-point spending have none: both need a screen position and a
# widget layout to mean anything, and a dedicated server has neither. A LAN client will drive
# equip changes through its own request, not a replayed mouse coordinate -- when that request
# exists, it gets its own recording and its own row, not this one reinstated.
#
# invdrag.rec is kept, unplayed by this suite, as the record of what P1.3d-4's storage-move once
# proved on a client-backed server. See plans/phase1/P1.4c-main-server-rewrite.md.
